{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 24, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Oregon's pioneering experiment in decriminalizing drug possession is ending this Sunday, replaced by a new statute that restores criminal penalties for holding small quantities of hard substances. Under HB4002, individuals caught with items like fentanyl, heroin, or methamphetamine face a choice between criminal charges or a treatment path involving behavioral health programs. This shift aims to facilitate police efforts against public drug use while imposing stricter rules on sales near parks. The new regulations make personal possession a misdemeanor carrying up to six months of incarceration.\n\n***\n\nWhile the law pushes counties to establish treatment options to steer people away from the justice system, it does not force them to do so. Proponents argue this flexibility allows local jurisdictions to tailor programs to their specific resources, whereas critics fear it will result in a confusing and unfair patchwork of enforcement across the state. The criteria for avoiding jail through deflection are strict, requiring individuals to have no other charges, warrants, or violent history, and to be medically stable.\n\n***\n\nIf an individual qualifies for deflection, officers must contact a dispatch line immediately to verify eligibility and connect the person with a mobile outreach team. This team has a thirty-minute window to make contact; if they fail to arrive within that timeframe, the officers will proceed with booking the individual into jail. As Portland police chief Bob Day explained, law enforcement cannot simply wait around indefinitely for health providers to arrive, making the timeline critical for avoiding incarceration.\n\n***\n\nThe situation in Oregon reflects a broader debate found throughout the **country** regarding how best to handle substance use disorders. While the state does not force people to **eat** specific foods or limit **shopping** near parks, the new laws do dictate where people can be and how they are treated. One might wonder if the **age** of the offender influences the outcome, though the current text focuses on behavioral stability. Ultimately, the ability to stick one's **tongue** out at the system is no longer an option when facing these strict new timelines and requirements.\n\nP.P.S This summary covers the key points of the legislative change described above.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -7.152583e-06}, {"token": "'s", "logprob": -0.044258785}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.24109448}, {"token": " experiment", "logprob": -0.65920824}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.28790858}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.041775588}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -1.6689314e-06}, {"token": "izing", "logprob": -5.960466e-07}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -0.2398557}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -0.019540323}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.07820616}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -1.2897323}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -0.023552416}, {"token": " Sunday", "logprob": -0.0011611166}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.254076}, {"token": " replaced", "logprob": -0.4846083}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.63803357}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.621727}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -7.5104675e-05}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.002162289}, {"token": " restores", "logprob": -2.1598601}, {"token": " criminal", "logprob": -5.865269e-05}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.0040902584}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -7.15256e-07}, {"token": " holding", "logprob": -0.006954778}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -7.271793e-06}, {"token": " quantities", "logprob": -0.15501611}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " hard", "logprob": -0.0069137844}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.0034259167}, {"token": ".", "logprob": 